<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/waves-and-sound">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    At the foundational level you met waves as ripples on a pond and wiggles on a rope. Now we make
    the description quantitative. A wave is energy in transit without transport of matter: the
    <em>shape</em> travels, the medium merely oscillates in place. Any wave in any medium — air,
    water, steel, or the vacuum of space — can be pinned down by three numbers and one equation:
    <strong>amplitude</strong> (how big each oscillation is), <strong>wavelength λ</strong> (how
    long one full cycle is, in metres), and <strong>frequency f</strong> (how many cycles pass a
    fixed point each second, in hertz). The equation binding them is <code>v = f·λ</code>: wave
    speed equals frequency times wavelength. It is the single most useful relation in this chapter,
    and everything from guitar strings to radio antennas is an application of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sound is our star example because you can both hear and feel it, and because it forces the most
    useful distinction in wave physics: transverse versus longitudinal. But we will also follow the
    same machinery upward into light and the electromagnetic spectrum, where the same three numbers
    describe colour, radio, X-rays and gamma rays.
  </p>

  <h2>The anatomy of a wave</h2>
  <p>
    Picture a single crest moving along a rope. The rope itself only moves up and down, at right
    angles to the wave's travel — this is a <strong>transverse</strong> wave. Shake the rope along
    its own length instead and you get squeezed and stretched regions moving along it — a
    <strong>longitudinal</strong> wave, where the oscillation is parallel to the travel direction.
    Sound in air is exactly this: alternating zones of compression (molecules bunched, pressure
    above normal) and rarefaction (molecules spread out, pressure below normal) marching away from
    the source. Water ripples are a mixture, which is why floating debris bobs in little circles
    rather than just up and down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The vocabulary is easiest on a graph of displacement against position. The
    <strong>amplitude</strong> is the height of a crest above the flat, undisturbed level — it
    measures how much energy the wave carries (for a string wave, energy goes as amplitude
    squared, so tripling the amplitude carries nine times the energy). The
    <strong>wavelength</strong> is the distance between two identical points of the pattern —
    crest to crest, or compression to compression. The <strong>frequency</strong> is how many
    complete cycles pass per second; its reciprocal is the <strong>period</strong>, T = 1/f, the
    time for one cycle. One full wavelength passes any point in one period, so the wave's speed
    must be wavelength divided by period: v = λ/T = f·λ.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example. A tuning fork vibrates at 440 Hz (concert A) and sound leaves it at 343 m/s.
    The wavelength in air is λ = v/f = 343/440 ≈ 0.78 m — about arm's length. Dip the fork in
    water? Sound would not travel through the fork's prongs at 343 m/s; in steel the speed is
    about 5,900 m/s, so the <em>same</em> 440 Hz vibration would launch a wave about 13.4 m long.
    Same frequency, different medium, different wavelength — remember that pattern; it returns
    below.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a wave carries matter along with it. Watch a floating ball as
  ripples pass: it bobs up and down and stays put. What travels is the pattern and its energy; the
  medium's particles oscillate about fixed positions. (A surfer is the exception that proves the
  rule — surfers ride the breaking part of the wave, where water genuinely does move shoreward.)</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two knobs, every wave — amplitude sets height, λ sets the stretch; the second curve lags in phase" widget="plotter" config={waveCfg} />

  <h2>What happens when waves meet the world</h2>
  <p>
    Four behaviours cover nearly everything a wave can do when its path changes, and they apply to
    sound and light alike.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reflection:</strong> the wave bounces off a boundary. Angle in equals angle out,
    measured from the surface's normal. This is an echo — a sound wave reflected from a cliff or
    wall.</li>
    <li><strong>Refraction:</strong> the wave crosses into a new medium, changes speed, and — if it
    hits the boundary at an angle — changes direction. Crucially, its <em>frequency does not
    change</em>: the source still shakes at the same rate, and the boundary must pass crests
    through at the rate they arrive. The wavelength adjusts instead, because v = f·λ with the new
    v. Sound refracts when it passes from cold air into warm air; light refracts entering water,
    which is why a straight straw looks bent at the surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Diffraction:</strong> the wave bends around obstacles and spreads through gaps —
    most noticeably when the gap is comparable to the wavelength. You hear someone round a corner
    (audible wavelengths run from centimetres to 17 metres, larger than doorways) but you cannot
    see them (light's wavelength is under a millionth of a metre).</li>
    <li><strong>Superposition:</strong> overlapping waves simply add, displacement by displacement.
    Crest on crest reinforces; crest on trough cancels.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Superposition deserves a slow look, because it explains effects that seem almost magical. Slide
    the phase gap φ between the two waves below: at φ = 0 the two waves are identical and the sum
    (red) has double amplitude — <strong>constructive interference</strong>. At φ = π (half a
    cycle) each crest of one lands on a trough of the other and the sum goes flat —
    <strong>destructive interference</strong>. Noise-cancelling headphones do precisely this in
    real time: a microphone samples the ambient noise and a speaker plays it back half a cycle out
    of phase, so the two sounds annihilate before reaching your eardrum.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="interference live — slide the phase gap from 0 (fully reinforced) to π ≈ 3.14 (fully cancelled)" widget="plotter" config={superCfg} />

  <p>
    Superposition between waves travelling in opposite directions produces a
    <strong>standing wave</strong>: fixed nodes (permanent cancellation) and antinodes (maximum
    swing). Plucked strings and blown pipes only sustain notes whose wavelength "fits" the
    instrument — an open guitar string, pinned at both ends, must have nodes at each end, which is
    why halving its length (pressing at the 12th fret) doubles the frequency and raises the pitch
    by one octave.
  </p>

  <h2>Sound, the pressure wave</h2>
  <p>
    Sound begins with something vibrating — a drumhead shoving air, vocal cords chopping your
    breath into puffs. The vibration pushes and pulls the air into those longitudinal compressions
    and rarefactions, and each little parcel of air hands the disturbance to its neighbours. Sound
    cannot travel through a vacuum: with no molecules, there is nothing to compress, which is why
    the film-star explosion is silent in space.
  </p>
  <p>
    How fast the disturbance travels depends entirely on the medium — not on how loud the sound is
    or how high its pitch. In air at 20 °C it is about 343 m/s, rising roughly 0.6 m/s per °C
    (warmer molecules move faster and pass the disturbance on sooner). In fresh water it is about
    1,480 m/s; in steel about 5,900 m/s. The rule of thumb: stiff, light media carry sound fast;
    dense-but-squishy media carry it slowly. Steel is 8 times denser than water, which should slow
    sound down, but it is also vastly stiffer — and stiffness wins, by a factor of about 16 in
    speed. (In a rigid solid like diamond, sound reaches 12,000 m/s.)
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> louder sounds travel faster, or high-pitched sounds overtake
    low ones. For ordinary sounds neither is true — all frequencies and amplitudes travel together
    at the medium's speed, which is why a distant marching band arrives as a chord rather than
    smearing out of tune. (At extreme amplitudes — explosions, sonic booms — the wave itself
    heats the air and the "fast parts" of the wave do overtake, steepening into a shock front. That
    is the exception, not the rule of everyday acoustics.)
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pitch is frequency.</strong> Human hearing runs from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz — ten
    octaves, and the top end decays with age and loud-music exposure, so a middle-aged adult may
    hear little above 15 kHz. Middle C is 262 Hz (wavelength ≈ 1.3 m in air); the top note of a
    piano, 4,186 Hz (wavelength ≈ 8 cm); a bat's hunting chirp reaches 100 kHz and beyond, with
    wavelengths of a few millimetres — small enough to echo off an insect. Below 20 Hz lies
    infrasound (earthquakes, elephants, wind turbines); above 20 kHz, ultrasound. Medical
    ultrasound scanners ping tissue at megahertz frequencies — a 3 MHz wave in soft tissue, where
    sound travels near 1,540 m/s, has a wavelength of about 0.5 mm, fine enough to resolve
    millimetre-scale structures in a fetus.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Loudness is amplitude</strong>, but our ears judge it on a logarithmic scale: each
    10-decibel step is a tenfold increase in intensity, though it sounds about twice as loud. A
    whisper near 30 dB, normal conversation near 60 dB, a bulldozer at 90 dB, a rock concert around
    110 dB — where damage to hearing begins in minutes — and 130 dB is the threshold of pain.
    Because the scale is logarithmic, a 60 dB conversation is not "twice" a 30 dB whisper but a
    thousand times the intensity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Echoes are just timing.</strong> You shout at a cliff and hear the echo 1.2 s later.
    The sound made a round trip, so the cliff is d = v·t/2 = 343 × 1.2 / 2 ≈ 206 m away. The same
    arithmetic drives sonar (a submarine pings and listens: a return 2.0 s after a downward ping
    toward a seabed, at 1,480 m/s in seawater, puts the bottom 1,480 × 2.0 / 2 = 1,480 m below) and
    the bats, which time chirps returning from insects with millisecond precision. Note the factor
    of 2 — forgetting it is the classic exam error.
  </p>

  <h2>Light and the electromagnetic family</h2>
  <p>
    Sound needs a medium; light does not. Light is an oscillation of electric and magnetic fields
    that regenerates each other as they travel, so it sails through vacuum at
    <strong>c = 299,792,458 m/s</strong> — about 880,000 times the speed of sound in air, which is
    why lightning arrives before its thunder: count the seconds between flash and boom and multiply
    by 340 to get the storm's distance in metres.
  </p>
  <p>
    Visible light is one narrow band of a continuous family that differs <em>only</em> in
    frequency and wavelength. From long to short: radio waves (metres to kilometres — FM radio at
    100 MHz has a 3 m wavelength, which is why car antennas are about that scale), microwaves
    (millimetres to metres — radar, Wi-Fi at 2.4 GHz is a 12.5 cm wave, and the 2.45 GHz oven
    band), infrared (700 nm to 1 mm — heat radiation, TV remotes), visible light (400–700 nm —
    violet to red), ultraviolet (10–400 nm — sunburn), X-rays (0.01–10 nm — bone imaging), and
    gamma rays (under 0.01 nm — nuclear decay). All of them travel at c in vacuum and all obey the
    same v = f·λ; an FM station and a dental X-ray are the same phenomenon at different frequencies.
  </p>
  <p>
    One wave behaviour of light is worth seeing here because it depends so directly on wavelength:
    <strong>dispersion</strong>. When light enters glass it slows (to roughly 2 × 10⁸ m/s), and the
    slowing — and hence the bending — depends slightly on colour: violet slows and bends more than
    red. A prism therefore spreads white light into a spectrum, and raindrops do the same job over
    the whole sky to make rainbows, violet on the inside edge of the arc, red on the outside.
    Refraction, lenses and mirrors get their own full treatment in the Optics chapter; the point
    here is that they are wave phenomena, governed by the same speed-frequency-wavelength bookkeeping
    as sound.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "wavelength" for light is something separate from colour. It is
  the colour — your eye's cone cells are frequency analysers, each tuned to a band of the same
  continuum the radio dial covers, just seven-plus octaves higher.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Doppler effect.</strong> If the source or listener moves, the received frequency
    shifts because each new crest is emitted from a different place. A siren approaching at 30 m/s
    compresses its wavefronts: a listener hears roughly f·v/(v − 30) = f × 343/313, about 10% sharp.
    As it passes and recedes the factor flips to 343/373, about 8% flat — the falling "eeee-ooooh".
    The same physics, applied to light, gives the redshift of receding galaxies; the American
    physicist Edwin Hubble's 1929 discovery that distant galaxies are redshifted in proportion to
    their distance is the observational foundation of the expanding universe.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>When the linear rule breaks.</strong> The claim "all sound in air travels at 343 m/s"
    assumes small-amplitude waves in still air. Blast waves from explosions travel faster than
    sound precisely because they compress the air so much that its properties change within the
    wave itself. And when a source moves <em>faster</em> than sound — a fighter jet at Mach 1.5, a
    bullet, the tip of a cracking whip — it outruns its own wavefronts, which pile into a cone
    trailing behind at angle arcsin(1/M) (M is the Mach number): the sonic boom. The boom is not a
    one-off event at the moment of breaking the barrier; the cone is dragged along continuously and
    everyone under the path hears it as the source passes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why the decibel is logarithmic — and what it hides.</strong> Hearing spans roughly 12
    orders of magnitude in intensity (10⁻¹² W/m² at threshold to about 1 W/m² at the pain
    threshold). A linear scale for loudness is useless, so decibels compare an intensity I against
    that threshold: 10·log₁₀(I/10⁻¹²) dB. The log also hides real damage: 85 dB for a full
    working day is the usual occupational limit, and every extra 3 dB halves the safe exposure
    time — 100 dB for 15 minutes already costs you some hearing permanently, because the hair
    cells of the inner ear do not regrow.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open territory.</strong> Two frequencies sounding together produce a third, slow
    "beat" at their difference (piano tuners listen for beats dying out as two strings converge);
    the cocktail-party effect — how the brain separates one voice from a wall of overlapping
    superposition — is still only partly understood; and rooms with "dead spots" are standing-wave
    nodes you can walk through, a reminder that even a single note is a field of cancellations
    waiting to be mapped. Take any two of the three numbers in v = f·λ, and the third is already
    fixed — that constraint is what makes acoustics, optics, radio engineering and seismology all
    speak the same language.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 16–17, Oscillatory Motion, Waves and Sound); HyperPhysics (Georgia State University), Sound Speeds and Decibel Scale; ISO 16 standard for concert pitch; CERN and NASA fact pages on the electromagnetic spectrum.</p>
